<p>A website conversion audit inspects the journey from an entry page to a defined customer action, such as an enquiry, booking or purchase. It identifies confirmed barriers and separates them from ideas that need testing. Start with the intended outcome and a reliable record of what completion means.</p>
<h2 id="what-is-a-conversion-audit">What does a conversion journey audit cover?</h2>
<p>Cover the entry promise, decision information, action controls, required inputs, error recovery and confirmation. Use the <a href="/cro-website-audit/">CRO measurement guide</a> for event definitions and experiment planning; this page focuses on inspecting the actual interface.</p>
<p>Record the URL, device, consent state and account conditions. A returning signed-in customer can encounter a different journey from a first-time visitor. Include both when they matter to the business.</p>
<h2 id="landing-page-analysis">What should the landing page establish?</h2>
<p>The landing page should explain the offer and match the promise that brought the visitor there. Compare the page with its ad, email or search-result context.</p>
<ul><li>Is the product or service and intended audience clear?</li><li>Can the visitor find material conditions, costs and eligibility?</li><li>Are claims backed by the evidence shown?</li><li>Are delivery, availability and next steps understandable?</li><li>Does the page remain usable while images, fonts and scripts load?</li></ul>
<p>Record specific gaps instead of assuming a longer page or a different colour will improve conversion. Check performance separately when a delay can be reproduced.</p>
<h2 id="cta-effectiveness">How should action controls be evaluated?</h2>
<p>Check that the label, destination and resulting state agree. “Download template” should provide the named file; “Request a quote” should not imply an immediate paid purchase.</p>
<p>Test keyboard activation, visible focus, touch use and the behaviour after repeated clicks. Place actions where visitors have enough information to decide. A sticky control must not hide content or focused elements.</p>
<p>The <a href="/website-audit-services/report-example/">AuditWeb report example</a> provides a real download workflow to inspect. Its presence does not establish a conversion-rate uplift.</p>
<h2 id="form-optimisation">Which form states need checking?</h2>
<ol><li>Read the purpose and commitment before entering information.</li><li>Check visible labels, required fields, suitable input types and autocomplete.</li><li>Try valid and invalid test values, including a correction after an error.</li><li>Confirm useful input remains available after a failed attempt.</li><li>Check the pending state and protection against accidental repeat submission.</li><li>Verify the confirmation and destination record through an agreed test procedure.</li></ol>
<p>W3C's <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/forms/">forms guidance</a> covers accessible structure and feedback. Removing a field can reduce effort, but retain information genuinely needed for the task. There is no fixed conversion loss per field.</p>
<h2 id="trust-signals">Which evidence belongs near a decision?</h2>
<p>Present accurate scope, prices, terms and relevant evidence. Check that testimonials have permission, ratings identify their source and certifications are current and applicable. Do not add a badge merely because it looks reassuring.</p>
<p>Distinguish an owned example from independent validation. Explain how supplied information will be used, and make contact and policy routes easy to find. A padlock or HTTPS connection does not certify the organisation or the whole application.</p>
<h2 id="checkout-flow">How should checkout be reviewed?</h2>
<p>Use an authorised test environment to inspect the full order path. Confirm totals, shipping options, taxes and other charges before the final commitment.</p>
<p>Check guest and account paths where offered, permitted payment methods, inventory changes, declined-payment feedback and recovery without losing the order. An account requirement may serve a legitimate product need; evaluate that need rather than removing it automatically.</p>
<p>Verify whether the confirmation corresponds to an actual test order and whether expected notifications arrive. Never place real orders or trigger real bookings solely to validate an audit page.</p>
<h2 id="ab-testing-recommendations">Which changes should be tested?</h2>
<p>Repair a confirmed broken route against its expected behaviour. Use a controlled experiment for uncertain changes to wording, layout or offer presentation when the available traffic supports a useful comparison.</p>
<p>State the hypothesis without an invented uplift: “A clearer quote-button label may reduce uncertainty about the next step.” Define the metric, eligible audience, guardrails and decision rule. Google explains relevant crawl considerations in its <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/website-testing">website-testing guidance</a>.</p>
<h2 id="measuring-improvement">How should improvement be measured?</h2>
<p>Repeat the same journey and compare matching definitions and periods. Record implementation dates and changes in audience, channel, consent or product availability that could affect the result.</p>
<p>Report completion alongside relevant downstream outcomes such as qualified enquiries, fulfilled orders or refunds. A higher submission count is not automatically higher business value. Mark small or confounded comparisons as inconclusive.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/resources/agency-audit-toolkit/">implementation tracker</a> to attach the correction, owner and retest evidence. The free HTML checker supplies source observations; it does not perform a live conversion audit.</p>
